Building {{ robot.name }} is safe when you follow a few simple rules. Read this
page before you order, print or assemble anything.

## Who can build this

This project is designed for builders **age 14 and up**. Younger makers are
very welcome — with an adult alongside for the battery, tools and small parts.

## Small parts and pinch points

- Screws, the caster ball and sensor boards are **small parts** — keep them
  away from young children and pets.
- Motors and wheels can **pinch fingers and pull in hair**. Keep hands clear of
  the wheels while the robot is powered, and switch it off before picking it up
  for adjustments.
- Freshly printed parts can have sharp edges. Deburr them with light sanding
  before assembly.

## The battery — read this twice

{{ robot.name }} runs from a single lithium cell, and lithium cells demand
respect:

- **We never ship lithium batteries.** Buy a **protected 18650 cell** from a
  reputable local supplier, together with a **quality single-cell USB-C
  charger**. "Protected" means the cell has a built-in cut-off against
  over-discharge and short circuits — it is worth the extra dollar.
- **Never charge a cell unattended** and never charge it overnight. Charge on
  a hard, non-flammable surface, away from anything that burns.
- Stop using any cell that is dented, punctured, swollen or gets hot at rest,
  and recycle it at a battery drop-off point — never in household trash.
- Mind the polarity when inserting the cell. Reversed cells can destroy the
  electronics and heat up fast.

## USB power

- While flashing and testing you can power the robot from a computer USB port
  — no battery needed. This is the safest way to do the first bring-up.
- Use a **data-capable USB-C cable** (many cheap cables are charge-only).
- Don't drive the motors hard on USB power alone: a laptop port can brown-out,
  which resets the robot mid-run. Use the battery for real driving.

With that covered, on to the fun part: [sourcing the parts](./source-parts/).
